What Data Should E-commerce Brands Monitor?
Price is important, but it is not the only information that can influence competitive analysis. A useful monitoring strategy may include several data points.
Product Price
The current selling price provides the foundation for price comparison.
Original and Discounted Price
Tracking both values can help businesses understand how aggressively competitors are using discounts.
Promotions and Coupons
Promotions can significantly change the effective customer price. Capturing available discounts and coupon information helps businesses understand competitive offers more accurately.
Product Availability
A competitor may have a lower price but limited stock. Monitoring availability provides additional context when comparing offers.
Seller Information
Multiple sellers may offer the same product at different prices. Seller-level information helps businesses understand which seller is driving the lowest observed price.
Product Variations
Different sizes, colors, quantities, models, or specifications can have different prices. Matching the correct variation is essential for accurate comparison.
Marketplace
The same product can have different prices and promotional conditions across marketplaces. Recording the marketplace allows businesses to analyze these differences separately.
Product Ratings and Reviews
Pricing decisions can sometimes be influenced by perceived product value. Ratings and review counts provide additional competitive context.
Shipping and Delivery Information
Delivery costs and speed can influence the overall attractiveness of an offer. Where relevant, these fields can be incorporated into competitive monitoring.
Collection Timestamp
A timestamp is particularly important for historical pricing.
Without knowing when a price was collected, it becomes difficult to analyze price movements accurately.
